      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats around a round table, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the total number of ways to seat the nine people around a round table.**\n   The number of ways to arrange \\( n \\) people in a circle is \\((n-1)!\\). Here, \\( n = 9 \\), so the total number of arrangements is:\n   \\[\n   (9-1)! = 8! = 40320\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the number of favorable arrangements where all four math majors sit in consecutive seats.**\n   Treat the four math majors as a single \"block\" or \"super person\". This means we now have 6 \"people\" to arrange around the table: the block of 4 math majors and the other 5 individuals (3 physics majors and 2 biology majors). The number of ways to arrange these 6 \"people\" in a circle is:\n   \\[\n   (6-1)! = 5! = 120\n   \\]\n   Within the block of 4 math majors, they can be arranged among themselves in \\(4!\\) ways:\n   \\[\n   4! = 24\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the total number of favorable arrangements is:\n   \\[\n   5! \\times 4! = 120 \\times 24 = 2880\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the probability.**\n   The probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats is the number of favorable arrangements divided by the total number of arrangements:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2880}{40320} = \\frac{1}{14}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{14}}\n\\]",
