      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we'll break it down into two main parts: calculating the total number of possible seating arrangements and calculating the number of favorable seating arrangements where all four math majors sit consecutively.\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the total number of possible seating arrangements\n\nSince the table is round, we can fix one person in one seat to remove the rotational symmetry. This leaves us with \\(8!\\) (8 factorial) ways to arrange the remaining 8 people.\n\n\\[\n8! = 40320\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the number of favorable seating arrangements\n\nWe need to consider the arrangement of the four math majors sitting consecutively. This means we can treat the block of four consecutive math majors as a single \"super person\". This reduces the problem to arranging 5 \"people\" (the super block of math majors, plus the 3 physics majors and the 2 biology majors).\n\nThe number of ways to arrange these 5 \"people\" is \\(5!\\):\n\n\\[\n5! = 120\n\\]\n\nWithin the \"super block of math majors\", there are \\(4!\\) ways to arrange the 4 math majors:\n\n\\[\n4! = 24\n\\]\n\nThus, the total number of favorable arrangements is:\n\n\\[\n5! \\times 4! = 120 \\times 24 = 2880\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the probability\n\nThe probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats is the ratio of the number of favorable arrangements to the total number of possible arrangements:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Probability} = \\frac{\\text{Number of favorable arrangements}}{\\text{Total number of arrangements}} = \\frac{2880}{40320} = \\frac{1}{14}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{14}}\n\\]",
