      "text": "To solve this problem, we can break it down into a few steps:\n\n1. Calculate the total number of ways to arrange 9 people around a round table.\n2. Calculate the number of favorable arrangements where all four math majors sit consecutively.\n3. Compute the probability by dividing the number of favorable arrangements by the total number of arrangements.\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the total number of ways to arrange 9 people around a round table\n\nThe number of ways to arrange \\( n \\) people around a round table is \\((n-1)!\\). For 9 people, this is:\n\\[\n(9-1)! = 8! = 40320\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the number of favorable arrangements where all four math majors sit consecutively\n\nTreat the group of four math majors as a single \"block\". This block can be arranged internally in \\(4!\\) ways. Now we have 6 \"blocks\" to arrange around the table (the math block plus the 3 physics majors and the 2 biology majors). The number of ways to arrange these 6 blocks around a round table is \\((6-1)! = 5! = 120\\). Since the math block can be arranged internally in \\(4!\\) ways, the total number of favorable arrangements is:\n\\[\n4! \\times 5! = 24 \\times 120 = 2880\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Compute the probability\n\nThe probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats is the number of favorable arrangements divided by the total number of arrangements:\n\\[\n\\frac{2880}{40320} = \\frac{1}{14}\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{14}}\n\\]",
